<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> freestyle is riding the terrain park and playing on the mountain: jumps, rails, spins, and riding switch. Freeride is riding natural terrain: powder, trees, steeps, and big open faces. All-mountain mixes both. Most riders, especially beginners and intermediates, are best served by an all-mountain board.</p></div>

<h2>The three styles</h2>
<h3>Freestyle</h3>
<p>Freestyle riders spend time in the terrain park, hitting jumps, boxes, rails, and halfpipes, and they treat the whole mountain like a playground with side hits, butters, and spins. Riding switch, with the other foot forward, is a big part of it.</p>
<h3>Freeride</h3>
<p>Freeriders seek out natural terrain: powder stashes, tree runs, steep chutes, and big open bowls. The focus is on speed, stability, and float rather than tricks.</p>
<h3>All-mountain</h3>
<p>All-mountain riding covers everything: groomers, some powder, a few park laps. It's how most people actually ride, and it's where most boards are designed to perform.</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Compare</th><th scope="col">Freestyle</th><th scope="col">All-mountain</th><th scope="col">Freeride</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
