<h2>Freestyle boards and setup</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>True twin shape:</strong> identical nose and tail, so the board rides the same forward or switch.</li>
  <li><strong>Softer flex:</strong> easier to press, butter, and land forgivingly.</li>
  <li><strong>Slightly shorter length:</strong> lighter swing weight for spins.</li>
  <li><strong>Centered, duck stance:</strong> toes angled outward on both feet for balance in both directions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Freeride boards and setup</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Directional shape:</strong> a longer nose than tail, often with the stance set back so the nose floats in powder.</li>
  <li><strong>Stiffer flex:</strong> stability at speed and on variable snow.</li>
  <li><strong>Slightly longer length:</strong> more float and a steadier ride.</li>
  <li><strong>Tapered or swallowtail shapes:</strong> some powder boards narrow the tail to sink it and lift the nose.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why most riders should start all-mountain</h2>
<p>An all-mountain board handles groomed runs, occasional powder, and a few park laps. You don't need a specialized setup until you know where you love to ride. Many riders later add a second board: a soft twin for park days or a directional board for storm days. To learn more about how profile changes the ride, read <%= link_to "camber vs rocker", guide_path("camber-vs-rocker") %>.</p>

<h2>How to choose</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Love jumps, rails, and spins? Go freestyle.</li>
  <li>Chase storms and ride trees? Go freeride and watch <%= link_to "where it's snowing", where_is_it_snowing_path %>.</li>
  <li>Want one board for everything? Go all-mountain.</li>
</ul>
<p>Then size it with the <%= link_to "snowboard size calculator", snowboard_size_chart_path %>, which adjusts for park and powder, and read <%= link_to "how to choose a snowboard", guide_path("best-snowboard") %>.</p>
<% faqs << { question: "What is freeride snowboarding?", answer: "Riding natural terrain such as powder, trees, steeps, and open bowls, with a focus on speed, stability, and float rather than park tricks." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "What is freestyle snowboarding?", answer: "Riding the terrain park and using the mountain as a playground: jumps, rails, boxes, halfpipes, spins, and riding switch." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Should a beginner get a freestyle or all-mountain snowboard?", answer: "Most beginners should get an all-mountain board, ideally with a forgiving rocker or hybrid profile. It handles every kind of terrain while you figure out what you like." } %>
